  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionCode": "book6-article-008-body",
      "heading": "Reading the Book VI structure",
      "anchor": "article-body",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The Second World War intensified total war and demonstrated that modern state capacity could be used for production, mobilization, control and mass violence. Genocide exposed the destructive use of administrative and technical capacity when restraints fail, while nuclear weapons changed the upper bounds of military escalation."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The sections separate capacity from legitimacy: greater capability does not make its use justified. The nuclear age is treated not as a prediction of future war, but as a historical shift in destructive capacity, deterrence structures and systemic risk."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This article distinguishes historical fact, historical reconstruction and structural interpretation in the manuscript. Later outcomes are not treated as inevitable at the time, and motives or causal claims not present in the manuscript are not added."
        }
      ]
    }
